{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1777735684574,"sessionID":"ses_216b23bddffewUUo9nz9v4yE7O","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_3ae15393977649779b7126c8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/work/prompt.txt"},"output":"<path>/work/prompt.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # push_swap (Rust binary, restricted std) — Benchmark Prompt\n2: \n3: ## Task\n4: \n5: Implement the **42 push_swap** project in **Rust**. Output is a single executable named `push_swap` that takes a list of integers as command-line arguments and prints a sequence of stack operations that sort those integers, using only the allowed instruction set. Build with `rustc` directly — **no Cargo, no `Cargo.toml`, no crates.io dependencies**. The Rust standard library (`std`) is available but with the restrictions in [Allowed / forbidden imports](#allowed--forbidden-imports) below.\n6: \n7: ## How push_swap works\n8: \n9: You manage two stacks, `a` and `b`. Stack `a` starts containing the input integers (top of the stack = first argument). Stack `b` starts empty. Your job is to print operations that, when applied in order, leave stack `a` sorted ascending (smallest on top) with stack `b` empty.\n10: \n11: ### Instruction set (case-sensitive, one per line, lowercase)\n12: \n13: | op   | effect |\n14: |------|--------|\n15: | `sa` | swap the top two elements of stack a (does nothing if a has < 2 elements) |\n16: | `sb` | swap the top two elements of stack b |\n17: | `ss` | `sa` and `sb` together |\n18: | `pa` | take the top element of b and push it onto a (does nothing if b is empty) |\n19: | `pb` | take the top element of a and push it onto b (does nothing if a is empty) |\n20: | `ra` | rotate a up: the first element becomes the last (does nothing if a has < 2) |\n21: | `rb` | rotate b up: same on b |\n22: | `rr` | `ra` and `rb` together |\n23: | `rra`| reverse rotate a: the last element becomes the first |\n24: | `rrb`| reverse rotate b: same on b |\n25: | `rrr`| `rra` and `rrb` together |\n26: \n27: Each operation must be printed on its own line, in lowercase, with no leading/trailing whitespace.\n28: \n29: ### Argument parsing rules\n30: \n31: - The integers may be passed as **separate arguments** (`./push_swap 3 1 4 1 5`) or as **one quoted string** (`./push_swap \"3 1 4 1 5\"`). Both forms must work; treat any whitespace-delimited tokens inside an argument as separate integers.\n32: - Each token must parse as a 32-bit signed integer. An optional leading `+` or `-` is allowed; otherwise digits only.\n33: - Duplicates are not allowed.\n34: - If the input is empty, print nothing and exit 0.\n35: - If the input is already sorted ascending, print nothing and exit 0.\n36: - On any input error (non-integer, overflow beyond i32 range, duplicate), print `Error\\n` to **stderr** and exit with non-zero status. Print nothing to stdout.\n37: - An argv slot that is the empty string (e.g. `./push_swap \"\"`) is malformed input — print `Error\\n` to **stderr** and exit non-zero.\n38: \n39: ### Operation count grading\n40: \n41: The harness enforces only the passing floor on each test size, but the actual op count is recorded so you can be ranked across the 42 subject's grading bands:\n42: \n43: | input         | grading bands (best → passing floor)                              |\n44: | ---           | ---                                                               |\n45: | 3 elements    | ≤ 3 (excellent) · ≤ 5 (passing floor)                             |\n46: | 5 elements    | ≤ 12 (excellent) · ≤ 16 (passing floor)                           |\n47: | 100 random    | ≤ 700 / ≤ 900 / ≤ 1100 / ≤ 1300 / ≤ 1500 (passing floor)          |\n48: | 500 random    | ≤ 5500 / ≤ 7000 / ≤ 8500 / ≤ 10000 / ≤ 11500 (passing floor)      |\n49: \n50: Hitting ≤ 1500 / ≤ 11500 is a pass. Lower is meaningfully better — the gap between 700 and 1500 ops on n=100 is the difference between a chunked / radix-style algorithm and a quadratic one.\n51: \n52: ## Working directory contract\n53: \n54: - Working directory is `/work`. Write all files at the root.\n55: - No subdirectories, no `Cargo.toml`, no `Cargo.lock`, no `.cargo/`.\n56: - Do not modify or remove `opencode.json` if you see it.\n57: \n58: ## Deliverables (at `/work` root)\n59: \n60: 1. `push_swap.rs` — single Rust source file with a regular `fn main() { ... }` entry point.\n61: 2. `Makefile` — builds `push_swap` from `push_swap.rs`.\n62: \n63: ## Build contract\n64: \n65: `make` (or `make all`) must produce the executable `push_swap` at the root of `/work`. The compile rule must be exactly:\n66: \n67: ```\n68: rustc --edition 2021 --crate-type bin -C opt-level=2 -D warnings -o push_swap push_swap.rs\n69: ```\n70: \n71: Makefile rules required: `all`, `$(NAME)`, `clean`, `fclean`, `re`. No unnecessary relinking. Zero warnings (`-D warnings` is mandatory).\n72: \n73: ## Allowed / forbidden imports\n74: \n75: The Rust standard library is available, but the surface you may use is deliberately tiny. Anything else you need, you build yourself on top of these.\n76: \n77: ### Allowed\n78: \n79: | use case | Rust item |\n80: |---|---|\n81: | storage          | `Vec<T>` and its methods (constructors, `push`, `pop`, `insert`, `remove`, `swap`, `len`, indexing, `iter`, `iter_mut`, `truncate`, `clear`) — `Vec` is your sole container |\n82: | exit code        | `std::process::exit` |\n83: | read argv        | `std::env::args` |\n84: | stdout output    | `println!` |\n85: | stderr output    | `eprintln!` |\n86: | integer parsing  | `i32::from_str_radix`, `<&str as core::str::FromStr>::parse::<i32>()` |\n87: \n88: Plus: primitive `&str` / `String` methods (split / trim / chars / bytes / etc.), basic control flow, integer arithmetic, and any helper functions / types **you write yourself** in `push_swap.rs`.\n89: \n90: ### Forbidden\n91: \n92: - **No other output path**. Specifically banned: `print!`, `eprint!`, `write!`, `writeln!`, `format!`, `std::fmt::*`, `std::io::Write`, `std::io::BufWriter`, `std::io::stdout()`, `std::io::stderr()`. Use `println!` / `eprintln!` exclusively, with literal-string formats — e.g. `println!(\"ra\")`, `eprintln!(\"Error\")`.\n93: - **No `VecDeque`** and no other container from `std::collections::*` — `BinaryHeap`, `BTreeMap`, `BTreeSet`, `HashMap`, `HashSet`. `Vec` is the only container. Build any deque / ring-buffer semantics yourself.\n94: - **No `Box`, no `Rc`, no `Arc`, no `RefCell`, no `Cell`.** No alternative heap allocations.\n95: - **No sort helpers**: `<[T]>::sort`, `sort_unstable`, `sort_by`, `sort_by_key`, `sort_unstable_by`, `sort_unstable_by_key`, `Vec::sort*`. Deriving the sort is the entire task.\n96: - **No external crates**, no `Cargo.toml`, no `Cargo.lock`, no `.cargo/`.\n97: - No `core::ptr::copy*`, `core::ptr::write_bytes`, `core::slice::from_raw_parts*`, `core::intrinsics::*`.\n98: - No `unsafe` blocks. Stay in safe Rust.\n99: - No global mutable state, no `static mut`.\n100: \n101: ### Other rules\n102: \n103: - The program must not panic on the documented inputs (no out-of-bounds indexing, no `unwrap()` on values that may legitimately be `None`, no overflow).\n104: - Output operations go to **stdout**; errors go to **stderr**.\n105: \n106: > The harness runs a static scan over `push_swap.rs` (after stripping comments and string literals) and reports any forbidden pattern as a **violation**, alongside the test results. Don't try to launder banned items through aliases or `use ... as`; the scan checks the raw text.\n107: \n108: ## Verification (the host does this automatically)\n109: \n110: The host runs `push_swap` on a battery of inputs, captures stdout, replays the operations on a simulated pair of stacks, and asserts that the final state of stack `a` equals the input sorted ascending and that stack `b` is empty. Test cases include:\n111: \n112: - empty argv\n113: - already sorted lists of various sizes\n114: - the smallest non-trivial cases (2, 3, 5 numbers)\n115: - random permutations of 100 and 500 distinct integers\n116: - duplicate / non-numeric / overflowing inputs (must exit non-zero with `Error\\n` on stderr)\n117: \n118: Stop when `make` succeeds, `./push_swap` runs without crashing, and the output of `./push_swap 3 1 2` is a valid sorting sequence.\n\n(End of file - total 118 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# push_swap (Rust binary, restricted std) — Benchmark Prompt\n\n## Task\n\nImplement the **42 push_swap** project in **Rust**. Output is a single executable named `push_swap` that takes a list of integers as command-line arguments and prints a sequence of stack operations that sort those integers, using only the allowed instruction set. Build with `rustc` directly — **no Cargo, no `Cargo.toml`, no crates.io dependencies**. The Rust standard library (`std`) is available but with the restrictions in [Allowed / forbidden imports](#allowed--forbidden-imports) below.\n\n## How push_swap works\n\nYou manage two stacks, `a` and `b`. Stack `a` starts containing the input integers (top of the stack = first argument). Stack `b` starts empty. Your job is to print operations that, when applied in order, leave stack `a` sorted ascending (smallest on top) with stack `b` empty.\n\n### Instruction set (case-sensitive, one per line, lowercase)\n\n| op   | effect |\n|------|--------|\n| `sa` | swap the top two elements of stack a (does nothing if a has < 2 elements) |\n| `sb` | swap the top two elements of stack b |\n| `ss` | `sa` and `sb` together |\n| `pa` | take the top element of b and push it onto a (does nothing if b is empty) |\n| `pb` | take the top element of a and push it onto b (does nothing if a is empty) |\n| `ra` | rotate a up: the first element becomes the last (does nothing if a has < 2) |","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"work/prompt.txt","time":{"start":1777735684559,"end":1777735684570}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The user wants me to read the file ./prompt.txt and follow its instructions. Let me read it first.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_de94dd1ca001B5yEoQvtWJOF3Y","sessionID":"ses_216b23bddffewUUo9nz9v4yE7O","messageID":"msg_de94dc4a5001qMVIcIsjIs8JVa"}}
