ep 1: top of a is 5, rank=4, bit0=0 → pb\na: 3 1 4 2\nb: 5\n\nStep 2: top of a is 3, rank=2, bit0=0 → pb\na: 1 4 2\nb: 3 5 (3 on top of 5)\n\nStep 3: top of a is 1, rank=0, bit0=0 → pb\na: 4 2\nb: 1 3 5\n\nStep 4: top of a is 4, rank=3, bit0=1 → ra\na: 2 4 (rotated: first became last)\nWait, ra rotates up: first element becomes last.\na was [4, 2], after ra: [2, 4]\n\nStep 5: top of a is 2, rank=1, bit0=1 → ra\na was [2, 4], after ra: [4, 2]\n\nNow push everything from b back to a:\npa: top of b (1) → top of a: a = [1, 4, 2], b = [3, 5]\npa: top of b (3) → top of a: a = [3, 1, 4, 2], b = [5]\npa: top of b (5) → top of a: a = [5, 3, 1, 4, 2], b = []\n\nWe're back to where we started! That doesn't seem right.\n\nHmm, I think the issue is that for bit 0, all elements happened to be either 0 or 1, and the algorithm should eventually sort them. Let me continue with more bits.\n\nActually wait, the problem is that after the bit-0 pass, I ended up with the same stack. That's because 3 out of 5 elements had bit0=0 (ranks 0, 2, 4), and 2 had bit0=1 (ranks 1, 3). After the pass, the bit=0 elements went to b, then came back on top. But the relative order within each group was preserved... hmm, but the final arrangement was the same as the start.\n\nThat can't be right. Let me retrace.\n\nAfter step 5: a = [4, 2] (only bit0=1 elements), b = [1, 3, 5] (bit0=0 elements, 1 on top)\n\nNow push all from b to a:\npa: a = [1, 4, 2], b = [3, 5]\npa: a = [3, 1, 4, 2], b = [5]\npa: a = [5, 3, 1, 4, 2], b = []\n\nYes, we're back to the original. This makes sense for bit 0 because:\n- In the original, the bit0=0 elements were at positions 0,1,2 (5,3,1) and bit0=1 at positions 3,4 (4,2)\n- After the pass, they should be reorganized so bit0=0 elements come first (since we're doing LSD radix and bit0=0 < bit0=1)\n\nBut the result is [5, 3, 1, 4, 2] which is the same as the original. That's because in the original, the bit0=0 elements (5,3,1) already came before the bit0=1 elements (4,2).\n\nWait, that IS sorted by bit 0! The elements with bit0=0 (ranks 0, 2, 4 → values 1, 3, 5) are before elements with bit0=1 (ranks 1, 3 → values 2, 4). Within each group, the order from the previous pass is maintained.\n\nSo after bit 0, the stack is sorted by bit 0: [5, 3, 1, 4, 2]. Let's verify: ranks are [4, 2, 0, 3, 1], bit 0 values are [0, 0, 0, 1, 1]. Yes, sorted by bit 0.\n\nNow bit 1:\na: [5, 3, 1, 4, 2], ranks: [4, 2, 0, 3, 1]\n\nStep 1: 5 (rank 4, bit1=0) → pb\na: [3, 1, 4, 2], b: [5]\n\nStep 2: 3 (rank 2, bit1=1) → ra\na: [1, 4, 2, 3]\n\nStep 3: 1 (rank 0, bit1=0) → pb\na: [4, 2, 3], b: [1, 5]\n\nStep 4: 4 (rank 3, bit1=1) → ra\na: [2, 3, 4]\n\nStep 5: 2 (rank 1, bit1=0) → pb\na: [3, 4], b: [2, 1, 5]\n\nNow a has [3, 4] (bit1=1), b has [2, 1, 5] (bit1=0, 2 on top).\n\nPush from b to a:\npa: a = [2, 3, 4], b = [1, 5]\npa: a = [1, 2, 3, 4], b = [5]\npa: a = [5, 1, 2, 3, 4], b = []\n\nHmm, that's [5, 1, 2, 3, 4] with ranks [4, 0, 1, 2, 3]. Bit 1 values: [0, 0, 0, 1, 1]. Hmm, but within bit1=0 group: ranks 4, 0, 1 (not sorted by bit 0). Wait, I think the issue is that after bit 1 pass, we should be sorted by bits 1:0 combined.\n\nLet me check: ranks = [4, 0, 1, 2, 3], combined bits 1:0 = [(4>>0)&3, (0>>0)&3, (1>>0)&3, (2>>0)&3, (3>>0)&3] = [0, 0, 1, 2, 3]. That's not sorted by bits 1:0.\n\nSomething's wrong. Let me retrace more carefully.\n\nAfter bit 0 pass, we had a = [5, 3, 1, 4, 2]. But wait, I made an error earlier. Let me redo bit 0.\n\na: [5, 3, 1, 4, 2], ranks: [4, 2, 0, 3, 1]\nb: []\n\nProcessing 5 elements for bit 0:\n\n1. top=5, rank=4, bit0=0 → pb. a=[3,1,4,2], b=[5]\n2. top=3, rank=2, bit0=0 → pb. a=[1,4,2], b=[3,5]  (3 on top of 5)\n3. top=1, rank=0, bit0=0 → pb. a=[4,2], b=[1,3,5]  (1 on top)\n4. top=4, rank=3, bit0=1 → ra. a=[2,4]\n5. top=2, rank=1, bit0=1 → ra. a=[4,2]\n\nNow a=[4,2], b=[1,3,5]\n\nPush all from b:\npa: a=[1,4,2], b=[3,5]\npa: a=[3,1,4,2], b=[5]\npa: a=[5,3,1,4,2], b=[]\n\nHmm wait, that's the same stack we started with. But let me check if it's sorted by bit 0:\n\nRanks in order: [4, 2, 0, 3, 1]\nBit 0: [0, 0, 0, 1, 1]\n\nYes, the bit0=0 elements (4,2,0) come before bit0=1 elements (3,1). So it IS sorted by bit 0.\n\nBut wait, I realize the issue. When I push from b back to a, the order gets reversed compared to what was in b. In b, we have [1, 3, 5] (1 on top). When we pa:\n- First pa: 1 goes to top of a. a = [1, 4, 2]\n- Second pa: 3 goes to top of a. a = [3, 1, 4, 2]\n- Third pa: 5 goes to top of a. a = [5, 3, 1, 4, 2]\n\nSo the bit0=0 elements end up in reverse order! They were [5, 3, 1] originally (top to bottom in a), pushed to b as [5, 3, 1] (5 at bottom, then 3, then 1 on top), and then popped back as [1, 3, 5] (1 first, then 3, then 5). So they end up in reverse: [5, 3, 1] → [1, 3, 5].\n\nWait no. Let me be really careful.\n\nOriginal a: 5 3 1 4 2 (top to bottom, 5 is on top)\n\nStep 1: pb(5). b gets 5 on top. a: 3 1 4 2. b: 5\nStep 2: pb(3). b gets 3 on top. a: 1 4 2. b: 3 5 (3 on top of 5)\nStep 3: pb(1). b gets 1 on top. a: 4 2. b: 1 3 5 (1 on top)\n\nSo b from top to bottom: 1 3 5\n\nPushing back:\npa(1): a: 1 4 2, b: 3 5\npa(3): a: 3 1 4 2, b: 5\npa(5): a: 5 3 1 4 2, b: []\n\nSo a from top to bottom: 5 3 1 4 2. This is the same as the original!\n\nThe bit0=0 elements went to b in order [5, 3, 1] (5 first to bottom of b, then 3 on top, then 1 on top). Coming back, 1 comes off first (goes to top of a), then 3, then 5. So they end up as 5 3 1 in a (5 at bottom, 3 above, 1 on top). That IS the same order they were originally.\n\nWait, originally a had 5 3 1 4 2 (top to bottom), and the bit0=0 elements were at positions 0,1,2: 5,3,1. After the operation, a has 5 3 1 4 2 again. The bit0=0 elements are still 5,3,1 and bit0=1 are 4,2. Same as original.\n\nOK so this particular example was already sorted by bit 0, so the bit 0 pass didn't change anything. Let me try an example where bit 0 isn't sorted.\n\nLet's use a: [2, 5, 1, 4, 3] (top to bottom)\nRanks: sorted [1,2,3,4,5], so 2→1, 