nitely do it in ≤ 2.\n\n**5 elements**: \n1. Find the 2 smallest elements, push them to b (using pb and possibly ra/rra to position them)\n2. Sort the 3 elements in a\n3. Push back from b to a, inserting each in the correct position\n\nThis is more complex. Let me think about a simpler approach for 5 elements.\n\nActually, for 5 elements, a common approach:\n1. Push 2 elements to b (pb pb)\n2. Sort the 3 remaining in a (at most 2 ops)\n3. For each element in b, calculate the best position in a and insert it (rotate a to the right position, pa, possibly rotate back)\n\nFor step 3, inserting one element into a sorted stack of size k requires at most k/2 rotations + 1 pa + k/2 rotations back. But we can optimize by choosing ra vs rra based on which is shorter.\n\nActually, for the insertion, we don't need to rotate back. We just need to find the right rotation to bring the correct position to the top, then pa, and the stack is still sorted (just rotated). Then for the next insertion, we find the right rotation again.\n\nWait, but the final state needs to be fully sorted (not rotated). So at the end, we'd need to rotate to bring the smallest to the top.\n\nLet me think about this differently. After sorting the 3 elements in a and having 2 in b:\n- a is sorted (ascending, smallest on top)\n- b has 2 elements\n\nTo insert an element from b into sorted a:\n1. Figure out where it should go\n2. Rotate a to bring that position to the top (or one above the top, since we'll pa)\n3. pa\n4. The stack might be rotated, but it's still sorted\n\nAfter inserting both elements, rotate a to bring the smallest to the top.\n\nThis should work. The total operations for 5 elements should be around:\n- 2 pb\n- ≤ 2 sort\n- insertion 1: up to 2 rotations + 1 pa = 3\n- insertion 2: up to 3 rotations + 1 pa = 4\n- final rotation: up to 3\n\nThat's 2 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 3 = 14 in the worst case. Hmm, that might exceed 12.\n\nLet me think of optimizations:\n- When pushing to b initially, try to push elements that are easier to insert later (i.e., close to their final position)\n- Use rra instead of ra when it's shorter\n- Combine operations where possible\n\nActually, for the 5-element case, a common 42 approach is:\n1. While a has > 3 elements, find the element with the smallest rank and push it to b (using ra/rra to bring it to the top, then pb)\n2. Sort the 3 remaining in a\n3. Push back from b: for each element, rotate a to the correct position and pa\n4. Final rotation to bring smallest to top\n\nWait, I think a better strategy for 5 elements might be to push the median or the smallest to b, not necessarily in any order.\n\nActually, let me just implement a decent algorithm and see if it fits within the bounds. For 5 elements, even if I don't get \"excellent\" (≤ 12), I need to pass (≤ 16).\n\nLet me go with a simpler approach: for n ≤ 5, use insertion sort style.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest approach for n ≤ 5 is:\n\nFor n = 2: if not sorted, sa\nFor n = 3: specialized cases\nFor n = 4 or 5: push (n-3) elements to b, sort 3 in a, insert back\n\nFor the insertion step, when a is sorted and we want to insert an element x from b:\n- If x < min(a), rotate so min is on top, then pa (x goes on top of min, which is correct since x < min)\n  Wait, no. If x < min(a), x should be at the bottom of a (since smallest on top). So we want x on top, and x < everything in a, so we rotate a so that the bottom is on top, then pa. Hmm, no.\n\nWait, the stack has smallest on top. So if x < min(a), x should go on top. Just pa.\nIf x > max(a), x should go at the bottom. Rotate a until max is on top (bottom-1 position), pa, then rotate.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The stack has smallest on top, largest on bottom. We want to insert x into the correct position.\n\nIf x is smaller than everything in a, x goes on top. Just pa.\nIf x is larger than everything in a, x goes at the bottom. We need to rotate to make room.\nOtherwise, x goes between two elements. We need to rotate a until the element just above x's position is on top, then pa.\n\nFor a stack of size k, the rotation cost is at most floor(k/2). Plus 1 pa.\n\nFor 5 elements:\n- Push 2 to b: 2 ops (pb pb)\n- Sort 3 in a: at most 2 ops\n- Insert first from b into a of size 3: at most 1 rotation + 1 pa = 2 ops\n- Insert second from b into a of size 4: at most 2 rotations + 1 pa = 3 ops\n- Final rotation to bring min to top: at most 2 ops\n\nTotal: 2 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 2 = 11 ops. That's ≤ 12! Great.\n\nBut wait, the \"push 2 to b\" part also has a cost if we need to rotate to find the right elements to push. Let me reconsider.\n\nFor 5 elements, a simple approach:\n1. Just push the top 2 to b: pb, pb. (2 ops)\n2. Sort 3 in a: at most 2 ops.\n3. Insert from b back: for each, find the right position, rotate, pa. \n   - First insert (into size 3): up to 1 rotation + 1 pa = 2 ops\n   - Second insert (into size 4): up to 2 rotations + 1 pa = 3 ops\n4. Final rotation: up to 2 ops\n\nBut wait, when we push the top 2 to b, the 2 elements in b might not be in the optimal order for insertion. We could choose which element to insert first.\n\nAlso, for the initial push, we could be smarter about which 2 elements to push (finding the smallest 2 or the ones that are easiest to insert back). But that adds complexity.\n\nLet me go with the simple approach for now and see if it works within the bounds.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully for n=5. The simple approach of pushing top 2 to b might not always work well. For example, if the top 2 elements are the median and the largest, they'd need a lot of rotations to insert back.\n\nA better approach: push elements to b such that the 3 remaining in a can be sorted efficiently. One way: push the 2 smallest (or 2 largest) to b. Then after sorting a, inserting back is easier because the elements in b are at the extremes.\n\nBut finding and pushing specific elements requires rotations. Let me estimate:\n- Finding the smallest element and bring