,2]. ✓\n\nGreat, all cases check out.\n\nNow for n=4 and n=5, I'll implement the push-to-b, sort-3, insert-back approach.\n\nFor n=4: push 1 to b, sort 3 in a, insert back.\nFor n=5: push 2 to b, sort 3 in a, insert back.\n\nThe key is how to insert back efficiently.\n\nLet me think about this. After sorting 3 in a, a is [0, 1, 2] (ranks, ascending, min on top). b has 1 or 2 elements.\n\nFor inserting from b into sorted a:\n\nFunction `insert_b_to_a(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>)`:\n1. x = b[b.len()-1]... wait, b[0] is the top.\n   x = b[0]\n2. Find the position where x should go in a.\n3. Rotate a to prepare for insertion.\n4. pa.\n\nFor step 2, since a is sorted ascending (min on top), find p such that:\n- If x < a[0]: p = 0 (insert at top)\n- If x > a[a.len()-1]: p = a.len() (insert at bottom)\n- Else: find p such that a[p-1] < x < a[p]\n\nFor step 3, to insert at position p:\n- Rotate a so that a[p] is at the top (ra p times, or rra (a.len()-p) times)\n- But wait, after rotating, pa puts x on top of a[p], which means x is above a[p] and below... hmm.\n\nActually, let me think about it differently. After we rotate and pa, we need the stack to still be sorted (possibly rotated).\n\nLet me try a concrete approach. After inserting, the stack should be sorted when viewed as a circular buffer. Then at the end, we rotate to bring min to top.\n\nFor position p (0 = top, n = bottom):\n- We want x to be at position p in the final sorted stack\n- Rotate a by p positions (ra p times), so a[p] becomes the top\n- pa puts x on top of a[p]\n\nWait, but x should be below a[p-1] and above a[p] (in terms of value). After rotating p times, a becomes [a[p], a[p+1], ..., a[n-1], a[0], a[1], ..., a[p-1]]. pa puts x on top: [x, a[p], a[p+1], ..., a[n-1], a[0], a[1], ..., a[p-1]].\n\nIn this stack, x is at position 0, a[p] is at position 1, and a[p-1] is at position n. The circular order is: x, a[p], a[p+1], ..., a[n-1], a[0], a[1], ..., a[p-1]. This IS sorted (ascending) as a circular buffer if a[p-1] < x < a[p].\n\nWait, actually it should be a[p-1] < x < a[p] for the insertion position. But in the circular buffer, a[p-1] comes after all the other elements, and x comes before a[p]. So the sequence is ..., a[p-1], x, a[p], ... which is ..., smaller, x, larger, ... That IS sorted.\n\nSo after insertion, the stack is sorted as a circular buffer. The position of the minimum element might have shifted. After all insertions, we rotate to bring the minimum to the top.\n\nOK, so the approach is:\n1. For each element in b (process top of b first):\n   a. Find position p where x should go in sorted a\n   b. Rotate a by min(p, n-p) positions (using ra or rra)\n   c. pa\n2. After all insertions, find the position of the minimum in a and rotate to bring it to the top\n\nWait, but for n=5 with 2 elements in b, after the first insertion, a has 4 elements and it's sorted as a circular buffer. For the second insertion, I need to find the position of x in the sorted circular buffer.\n\nFinding the position of x in a sorted circular buffer:\n- The minimum element is somewhere in the stack\n- The elements are sorted starting from the minimum\n- Find where x fits\n\nThis is a bit more complex. Let me think...\n\nActually, for small n (up to 5), I can just do a simple search. For each possible insertion position in a, check if it would result in a sorted circular buffer.\n\nAlternatively, I can just find the minimum, treat the circular buffer as a linear sorted array starting from the minimum, and find the position.\n\nOr, I can normalize the stack (rotate so min is on top) before each insertion. This adds some operations but simplifies the logic.\n\nFor n=5, the overhead of normalizing before each insertion might push us over the op limit. Let me calculate:\n\nPush 2 to b: 2 ops (pb, pb)\nSort 3 in a: up to 2 ops\nNormalize a (bring min to top): up to 1 op (since a has 3 elements)\nInsert first: find position, rotate (up to 1 ra or rra for 3 elements), pa, normalize (up to 2 ra or rra for 4 elements): up to 1+1+2 = 4 ops\nInsert second: find position, rotate (up to 2 ra or rra for 4 elements), pa, normalize (up to 2 ra or rra for 5 elements): up to 2+1+2 = 5 ops\n\nTotal: 2 + 2 + 1 + 4 + 5 = 14 ops. That's ≤ 16 (passing floor) but > 12 (excellent).\n\nHmm, to get ≤ 12, I need to be smarter. Let me think about optimizations.\n\nOne optimization: instead of normalizing after each insertion, just normalize at the end. When finding the insertion position for a circular sorted buffer, I need to account for the rotation.\n\nAnother optimization: when pushing to b, choose which elements to push to minimize the total cost. For example, push elements that are at the extremes (smallest or largest) to b, so they're easy to insert back.\n\nLet me think about the optimal strategy for n=5 more carefully.\n\nFor n=5 with ranks [0,1,2,3,4]:\n- We want to push 2 elements to b\n- The best elements to push are probably those that require the fewest operations to handle\n\nActually, for the 42 push_swap project, the common approach for 5 elements is:\n1. Find the 2 smallest (or some other specific elements) and push them to b\n2. Sort the remaining 3\n3. Push back from b\n\nBut the exact strategy depends on the initial configuration. Let me just implement a reasonable approach and see if it passes.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just implement the radix sort for all sizes and see what happens. For n=3, the radix sort would use 2 bits and about 9 ops. That's above the 5-op limit. For n=5, 3 bits and about 22 ops, way above 16.\n\nSo I need specialized algorithms for n ≤ 5. Let me implement them carefully.\n\nFor n ≤ 3: direct cases (as above)\nFor n = 4: push 1, sort 3, insert\nFor n = 5: push 2, sort 3, insert\n\nFor the insertion part, let me implement a function that inserts an element from b into sorted (but possibly rotated) stack a.\n\nTo find where x goes in a sorted circular buffer a:\n- Find the minimum element in a and its position\n- The sorted sequence is a[min_pos], a[(min_pos+1)%n], ..., a[(min_pos+n-1)%n]\n- Find where x fits in this sequence\n- The position in the actual stack is (min_pos + offset) % n\n\nThen rotate to that position and pa.\n\nThis is O(n) per insertion, which is fine for small n.\n\nLet me code this up now. I'll implement everything in a single push_swap.rs file.\n\nActually, let me think about an even simpler approach for n=4 and n=5. What if I just simulate different strategies and pick the best one?\n\nFor n=4, there are only 4! = 24 permutations. I can try:\n- Push each of the 4 possible elements to b (4 choices)\n- Sort the remaining 3 (at most 2 ops)\n- Insert back (at most a few ops)\n- Find the total cost and pick the minimum\n\nFor n=5, there are 5! = 120 permutations. I can try pushing different pairs to b and pick the best.\n\nBut this requires simulating the operations without printing, counting them, and then printing the best sequence. I need a simulation framework.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest approach is to have a simulation function that takes the initial stack state, a list of operations, and returns the final state. Then I can try different operation sequences and find the shortest one.\n\nBut implementing BFS for n=5 might be overkill. Let me just implement a reasonable greedy approach and see if it works.\n\nOK, let me just start coding. I'll implement:\n\n1. Argument parsing and validation\n2. Ranking\n3. Specialized algorithms for n ≤ 3\n4. Insertion sort approach for n = 4, 5\n5. Radix sort for n > 5\n\nHere's my plan for the code structure:\n\n```rust\nuse std::env;\nuse std::process::exit;\n\nfn main() {\n    let nums = parse_args();\n    let n = nums.len();\n    if n == 0 { return; }\n    \n    // Check if sorted\n    if is_sorted(&nums) { return; }\n    \n    // Rank\n    let ranks = rank(&nums);\n    \n    // Sort\n    let mut a: Vec<i32> = ranks.clone();\n    let mut b: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n    \n    match n {\n        2 => { p