med input — print Error\\n to stderr and exit non-zero.\"\n\nI think the intent is:\n- No arguments (just `./push_swap`): print nothing, exit 0\n- Arguments provided but one of them is empty string: Error\n- Arguments provided, all valid integers: proceed\n- Arguments provided, some invalid: Error\n\nLet me handle it as follows:\n1. If no args (len <= 1): exit 0\n2. For each arg (from index 1):\n   a. If arg is empty: Error\n   b. Split by whitespace, parse each token\n   c. If any token fails to parse as i32: Error\n   d. If any duplicate: Error\n3. If no integers parsed (all args were whitespace-only but non-empty): This is an edge case. I'll treat it as Error since the user provided arguments but no valid integers.\n\nActually, for the case where an arg is non-empty but produces no tokens after splitting, I think it should be an error. For example, `./push_swap \"   \"` provides an argument \"   \" which is not empty but contains only whitespace. This doesn't contain any integers, so it should be an error.\n\nBut wait, `\"   \".split_whitespace()` gives no tokens, and we wouldn't parse any integers from it. Should we treat this as \"no input\" (exit 0) or as \"invalid input\" (Error)?\n\nI think the safest interpretation is: if there are arguments (args.len() > 1) but no integers were parsed, that's an error. Because the user clearly intended to provide input but it was malformed.\n\nHmm, but the spec says \"An argv slot that is the empty string\" specifically is malformed. It doesn't mention other cases. I'll go with: if any argument slot is empty, Error. Otherwise, process normally. If no integers result, exit 0 (same as empty input).\n\nActually, that doesn't feel right either. Let me just handle it pragmatically:\n- If args.len() <= 1: exit 0\n- If any arg is empty: Error\n- Parse all tokens from all args\n- If any token fails to parse: Error\n- If duplicates: Error\n- If no tokens (all args were whitespace-only): exit 0 (or Error? I'll go with exit 0 to be s