c\", etc. It also rejects overflow (e.g., \"2147483648\"). Does it reject things like \"++3\" or \"3a\"? Yes, it does. Does it reject leading zeros like \"007\"? No, \"007\".parse::<i32>() = Ok(7). Is that OK per the spec? The spec says \"digits only\" after the optional sign, so \"007\" is just digits. It should be fine.\n\nBut wait, what about \"++3\" or \"+-3\"? These contain non-digit characters, so they should be rejected. And `parse::<i32>()` does reject them. Good.\n\nWhat about just \"+\" or \"-\"? These are not valid i32 strings, so `parse::<i32>()` returns Err. Good.\n\nWhat about very large numbers like \"999999999999999999999\"? `parse::<i32>()` returns Err (overflow). Good.\n\nOK, I think `parse::<i32>()` handles all the validation we need, except for duplicates and empty arguments.\n\nWait, there's one more thing. The spec says the integer must be a \"32-bit signed integer\". So the range is -2147483648 to 2147483647. `parse::<i32>()` handles this correctly.\n\nBut I also need to handle the case where a number with a leading '+' is provided, like \"+42\". `parse::<i32>()` handles this correctly (returns Ok(42)). Good.\n\nOK, let me now write the complete code. I'll be very careful.\n\n```rust\nuse std::env;\nuse std::process::exit;\n\nfn main() {\n    let args: Vec<String> = env::args().collect();\n    let mut nums: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n    \n    if args.len() <= 1 {\n        exit(0);\n    }\n    \n    for i in 1..args.len() {\n        let arg: &str = &args[i];\n        if arg.is_empty() {\n            eprintln!(\"Error\");\n            exit(1);\n        }\n        let mut found_token = false;\n        for token in arg.split_whitespace() {\n            found_token = true;\n            match token.parse::<i32>() {\n                Ok(n) => {\n                    for j in 0..nums.len() {\n                        if nums[j] == n {\n                            eprintln!(\"Error\");\n                            exit(1);\n                        }\n                    }\n                    nums.push(n);\n                }\n                Err(_) => {\n                    eprintln!(\"Error\");\n                    exit(1);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        if !found_token && !arg.is_empty() {\n            // Non-empty arg with only whitespace - no valid integers\n            // Treat as error? The spec doesn't explicitly say.\n            // I'll not error here; if no tokens were found in this arg, it's just whitespace.\n        }\n    }\n    \n    let n = nums.len();\n    if n == 0 {\n        exit(0);\n    }\n    \n    // Check if already sorted ascending (smallest on top = nums[0] < nums[1] < ...)\n    let mut is_sorted = true;\n    for i in 1..n {\n        if nums[i - 1] >= nums[i] {\n            is_sorted = false;\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n    if is_sorted {\n        exit(0);\n    }\n    \n    // Rank the numbers\n    let ranks = rank(&nums);\n    \n    // Create stacks\n    let mut a: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n    let mut b: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n    for i in 0..n {\n        a.push(ranks[i]);\n    }\n    \n    // Sort\n    match n {\n        2 => { println!(\"sa\"); }\n        3 => { sort_3(&mut a); }\n        4 | 5 => { sort_small(&mut a, &mut b); }\n        _ => { radix_sort(&mut a, &mut b); }\n    }\n}\n\nfn rank(nums: &Vec<i32>) -> Vec<i32> {\n    let n = nums.len();\n    let mut idx: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\n    for i in 0..n {\n        idx.push(i);\n    }\n    // Selection sort on indices\n    for i in 0..n {\n        let mut min_j = i;\n        for j in (i + 1)..n {\n            if nums[idx[j]] < nums[idx[min_j]] {\n                min_j = j;\n            }\n        }\n        if min_j != i {\n            let tmp = idx[i];\n            idx[i] = idx[min_j];\n            idx[min_j] = tmp;\n        }\n    }\n    // idx is now sorted: nums[idx[0]] <= nums[idx[1]] <= ...\n    let mut ranks: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n    for _ in 0..n {\n        ranks.push(0);\n    }\n    for i in 0..n {\n        ranks[idx[i]] = i as i32;\n    }\n    ranks\n}\n\nfn sa(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if a.len() >= 2 {\n        let tmp = a[0];\n        a[0] = a[1];\n        a[1] = tmp;\n    }\n    println!(\"sa\");\n}\n\nfn sb(b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if b.len() >= 2 {\n        let tmp = b[0];\n        b[0] = b[1];\n        b[1] = tmp;\n    }\n    println!(\"sb\");\n}\n\nfn pa(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if !b.is_empty() {\n        let val = b.remove(0);\n        a.insert(0, val);\n    }\n    println!(\"pa\");\n}\n\nfn pb(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if !a.is_empty() {\n        let val = a.remove(0);\n        b.insert(0, val);\n    }\n    println!(\"pb\");\n}\n\nfn ra(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if a.len() >= 2 {\n        let val = a.remove(0);\n        a.push(val);\n    }\n    println!(\"ra\");\n}\n\nfn rb(b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if b.len() >= 2 {\n        let val = b.remove(0);\n        b.push(val);\n    }\n    println!(\"rb\");\n}\n\nfn rra(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if a.len() >= 2 {\n        let val = a.pop().unwrap(); // Safe because len >= 2\n        a.insert(0, val);\n    }\n    println!(\"rra\");\n}\n\nfn rrb(b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if b.len() >= 2 {\n        let val = b.pop().unwrap(); // Safe because len >= 2\n        b.insert(0, val);\n    }\n    println!(\"rrb\");\n}\n```\n\nWait, I'm using `unwrap()` on the `pop()`. The spec says \"no `unwrap()` on values that may legitimately be `None`\". Since I check `len() >= 2` before calling `pop()`, the pop will always return Some. So `unwrap()` is safe here. But the static scanner might flag `unwrap()`. Let me use a different approach.\n\n```rust\nfn rra(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if a.len() >= 2 {\n        let last_idx = a.len() - 1;\n        let val = a[last_idx];\n        a.remove(last_idx);\n        a.insert(0, val);\n    }\n    println!(\"rra\");\n}\n```\n\nThis avoids `unwrap()` but uses `remove`. That should be fine.\n\nActually, wait. `a.remove(last_idx)` on the last element is O(1) since there are no elements after it to shift. And `a.insert(0, val)` is O(n). But for n ≤ 500, this is fine.\n\nHmm, but for the radix sort, I'm doing O(n * bits) operations, each of which might involve O(n) insert/remove. That's O(n^2 * bits) total. For n=500, that's 500^2 * 9 = 2,250,000 operations, which should be fast enough.\n\nLet me also think about whether the `remove` and `insert` operations are allowed. The spec says `Vec<T>` methods including `insert` and `remove` are allowed. Good.\n\nNow let me implement the sort_3 function. I need to handle all 6 permutations of 3 elements.\n\n```rust\nfn sort_3(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let (x, y, z) = (a[0], a[1], a[2]);\n    \n    if x < y && y < z {\n        // [0,1,2] sorted - do nothing\n    } else if x < y && y > z && x < z {\n        // [0,2,1] - sa, ra\n        // Wait, sa on [0,2,1] gives [2,0,1]. Then ra gives [0,1,2]. That works.\n        // But actually: the condition x < y && y > z && x < z means x < z < y.\n        // Example: [0, 2, 1]. sa → [2, 0, 1]. ra → [0, 1, 2]. ✓\n        sa(a);\n        ra(a);\n    } else if x > y && x < z {\n        // [1,0,2] - y < x < z\n        sa(a);\n    } else if x > z && y > z && x < y {\n        // [1,2,0] - z < x < y\n        rra(a);\n    } else if x > y && x > z && y < z {\n        // [2,0,1] - y < z < x\n        ra(a);\n    } else {\n        // [2,1,0] - x > y > z\n        sa(a);\n        rra(a);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to double-check my conditions. For ranks 0,1,2:\n\n[0,1,2]: x=0, y=1, z=2. x<y, y<z. Sorted.\n[0,2,1]: x=0, y=2, z=1. x<y, y>z, x<z. → sa, ra\n[1,0,2]: x=1, y=0, z=2. x>y, y<z, x<z. → sa\n[1,2,0]: x=1, y=2, z=0. x<y, y>z, x>z. → rra\n[2,0,1]: x=2, y=0, z=1. x>y, y<z, x>z. → ra\n[2,1,0]: x=2, y=1, z=0. x>y, y>z, x>z. → sa, rra\n\nLet me verify each:\n1. [0,1,2]: ✓ (no ops)\n2. [0,2,1]: sa → [2,0,1], ra → [0,1,2] ✓\n3. [1,0,2]: sa → [0,1,2] ✓\n4. [1,2,0]: rra → [0,1,2] ✓\n5. [2,0,1]: ra → [0,1,2] ✓\n6. [2,1,0]: sa → [1,2,0], rra → [0,1,2] ✓\n\nGreat, all correct!\n\nNow for sort_small (n=4 or 5):\n\n```rust\nfn sort_small(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let n = a.len();\n    let push_count = n - 3;\n    \n    // Push (n-3) elements to b\n    for _ in 0..push_count {\n        pb(a, b);\n    }\n    \n    // Sort the 3 elements in a\n    sort_3_no_print(a);\n    // Wait, sort_3 prints operations. I should use the same function.\n    sort_3(a);\n    \n    // Insert elements from b back into a\n    while !b.is_empty() {\n        insert_b_to_a(a, b);\n    }\n    \n    // Rotate a so minimum is on top\n    rotate_min_to_top(a);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but sort_3 is already printing. And my operation functions (sa, ra, etc.) also print. So I don't need separate print logic.\n\nBut wait, for sort_3, the function modifies the stack AND prints. If I call it within sort_small, it'll print the operations for sorting the 3 elements. That's fine.\n\nBut there's a problem: sort_3 assumes a has exactly 3 elements. After pushing (n-3) elements to b, a should have exactly 3 elements. Good.\n\nNow I need to implement insert_b_to_a and rotate_min_to_top.\n\n```rust\nfn insert_b_to_a(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let x = b[0]; // top of b\n    let n = a.len();\n    \n    // Find the position where x should go in sorted a\n    // a is sorted ascending (min on top), possibly rotated\n    \n    // Find the minimum in a and its position\n    let mut min_pos = 0;\n    let mut min_val = a[0];\n    for i in 1..n {\n        if a[i] < min_val {\n            min_val = a[i];\n            min_pos = i;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Now a, when viewed starting from min_pos, is sorted ascending\n    // Find where x goes\n    let pos = find_insert_pos(a, x, min_pos);\n    \n    // Rotate a to bring a[pos] to the top\n    // We can use ra (pos times) or rra (n - pos times)\n    if pos <= n - pos {\n        for _ in 0..pos {\n            ra(a);\n        }\n    } else {\n        for _ in 0..(n - pos) {\n            rra(a);\n